import type { V205BookData } from '../types';

export const DATA: V205BookData = {
  book: "2 John",
  display: "Second John",
  chapters: {
    "1": {"chapter":1,"intro":[{"kind":"para","text":"In John’s last letter to these churches, he did not shy away from their most needed ministry, which seemed lacking if he had to talk so much about it. I feel as if they knew the love of God, but they didn’t harness that love to flow out to others. Perhaps they knew they had life because of the sacrifice of Christ; perhaps they understood that Christ’s light gifts man with purpose and vision; perhaps they even knew that Christ gave His own life because of the love God has for the world. However, it seems that these people didn’t know how to utilize the life, light, and love of Christ properly towards the people they were around the most—the church."},{"kind":"para","text":"John is going to go even deeper in this second letter (and even deeper again in the third). He is still going to talk about the same needs as before, however, with a clearer view of how to utilize it in their specific situation. As we read, we will see that John wants the church to focus on how to use love and truth right in the middle of pushback and conflict. These are hard things to do when someone is mistreating you."}],"previously":[],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":3,"title":"Love & Truth","entries":[{"start":1,"end":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"We will find out a little more about “the elect lady” in a moment. Obviously, John is writing this as a very personal letter to this woman for a reason. He starts off with humble greetings. He says, “I am just an elder… and I’m writing to you because I love you in the truth (or the Word of God).” John could have called himself “The Apostle” or he could have said, “The Wise,” but he chose “the elder.” An elder (in the church) is usually just an older person who has experienced some things in his life of ministry. The elders of a church know a thing or two about problems and solutions. The truly faithful elders of any church are good people to talk to and get life advice from. Most of them anyway. They must know the truth of God’s Word if they are going to be trusted for advice. John did not use authority to prove his position; he used age and time."},{"kind":"para","text":"I think it’s also important to note the “but also all they that have known the truth…” It’s not just John that loves the people of the church; it’s everyone that also has the love of God. Even if leaders in the church do not love properly, it does not give anyone else the right to follow suit and reject the love they are supposed to be giving to others as well. Why? Why can’t we treat others the way they are treating us? Why can’t we hurt them in return and shun love just like them? Well, the next verse actually tells us:"}]},{"start":2,"end":2,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The reason we must focus our attention in life and faith on love is because it exemplifies the truth. If we do not have love in our life or faith, no one will take your preaching and teaching seriously. They won’t care if you are saying the things that amount to absolute wisdom and necessity if they don’t first know that you love them (and others)."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Though I speak with the tongues of men and of angels, and have not charity, I am become as sounding brass, or a tinkling cymbal.","ref":"1 Corinthians 13:1"},{"kind":"para","text":"Without love, nothing will resonate with the people we are talking to. They simply won’t care. Not because you are not right, but because you are not nice. People can tell when you are just saying something to say something, and when you actually care about them."},{"kind":"para","text":"Based on the first two verses in this letter, you can tell that John means what he says about the people (and particularly the elect lady)."}]},{"start":3,"end":3,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"From what I’ve gathered, based on the last book and now as he really emphasizes the here, the people of the church are slowly creeping away from the truth that Jesus is God in the flesh. I do believe that is why he made such a strong statement about Jesus being our focal point of love, light, and life. Here, he emphasizes two things:"},{"kind":"para","text":"First, grace, mercy, and peace come from God the Father if anyone is looking for it. I think a lot of people want world grace, world mercy, and world peace. They might get little glimpses of what that could be here and there, but they will never see it in their lifetime. That is a fact of the matter. No matter how long a man may live, he will never find world grace, mercy, or peace. You can only find it in eternity if you are willing to look. One day, we will pass on from this life and see true grace, mercy, and peace, but until then, we should be searching for it in our own individual lives, but that can only come from the Lord. You cannot find it on Earth."},{"kind":"para","text":"Second, John emphasizes what we should all understand at this point: Jesus Christ is also Lord and the Son of the Father (in truth and in love). Again, truth and love will be a continual theme for John, but it’s found only in Jesus Christ. If you want grace, mercy, and peace, go to the Lord. If you want truth and love, go to the Lord. Grace, mercy, and peace are displayed by the promises of God. Truth and love are provenly kept by the sacrifice of Jesus Christ. If you didn’t believe John about this in the last letter (even with the six witnesses that were pronounced in the last chapter), it is highly unlikely you will ever believe what John is saying about grace, mercy, peace, truth, or love in this letter. A man who doesn’t believe the Lord in this matter will never know what true genuine grace feels like. He will never know what ultimate mercy is. He will never live in peace. John has already talked about how love and truth are not in people who don’t know the God of love and truth. God IS love, and He IS truth."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Beloved, let us love one another: for love is of God; and every one that loveth is born of God, and knoweth God.","ref":"1 John 4:7"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Jesus saith unto him, I am the way, the truth, and the life: no man cometh unto the Father, but by me.","ref":"John 14:6"},{"kind":"para","text":"You can't have either one of them without God."}]}]},{"start":4,"end":11,"title":"Christ's Doctrine","entries":[{"start":4,"end":4,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"We are going to read in the 4th verse of the next letter something almost perfectly similar. Honestly, I think the next letter’s version of this verse is even better! John was happy to hear that there are people in the church who started following the Lord and walking in the truth. Perhaps it was from his last letter that he sent that got them to change course and start loving each other more appropriately. Of course, that doesn’t mean that everything is fine now. In fact, John wouldn’t have to write another letter if things were okay."},{"kind":"para","text":"Most of the time, if the Apostles are writing a letter (it seems anyway), it’s about something that they need to help to fix. I’m sure there are letters from the Apostles out in the world that talk more about the weather and how great life is, but the preserved letters we got are the ones that talk about encouragement and help. These are not letters of disdain and hurt; they are letters of hope and action. That is the best way to look at it."},{"kind":"para","text":"I think a lesson for us to learn (when writing letters) is what the people are doing right before you start talking about what they are doing wrong. That is what all the Apostles seem to do anyway. Even in Revelation, as Jesus tells John what to write, Jesus starts off with what they are doing right before talking about what He has against them:"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"I know thy works, and thy labour, and thy patience, and how thou canst not bear them which are evil: and thou hast tried them which say they are apostles, and are not, and hast found them liars: And hast borne, and hast patience, and for my name's sake hast laboured, and hast not fainted. Nevertheless I have somewhat against thee, because thou hast left thy first love.","ref":"Revelation 2:2-4"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And unto the angel of the church in Smyrna write; These things saith the first and the last, which was dead, and is alive;","ref":"Revelation 2:8"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And unto the angel of the church in Sardis write; These things saith he that hath the seven Spirits of God, and the seven stars; I know thy works, that thou hast a name that thou livest, and art dead.","ref":"Revelation 3:1"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"I know thy works: behold, I have set before thee an open door, and no man can shut it: for thou hast a little strength, and hast kept my word, and hast not denied my name.","ref":"Revelation 3:8"},{"kind":"para","text":"I think we get the point. It is wise to start letters off with what people are doing well. Again, people know if you genuinely love them or not. If John started his letters off full swinging, these people would have stopped listening (or reading) almost immediately. They would have said, “John doesn’t really care. He just wants to cause more hurt to us. Throw the letter away.” I wonder how many people we have turned away because we approached them with facts instead of love first."}]},{"start":5,"end":5,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Perhaps the elect lady was actually telling them to ignore John’s advice from the last letter. John seems to be almost pleading with this woman. He wants her to not just teach love towards one another but to also be loving towards others. I think that is a great flaw in the modern church, honestly. Too many pastors and leaders tell others what to do without actually doing it themselves. They don’t have the desire to actually get involved."},{"kind":"para","text":"I heard about one pastor who was setting up a soul-winning event. He had all the logistics planned out and told everyone what their roles would be for this event. Someone finally asked him, “So what are you going to do?” He said, “I’m going to go home.” The other man was appalled, “Why aren’t you going to be at the event that you are planning?” “Because,” he said, “God tells me what to tell you guys to do, and you do it. I don’t have time for something like this. All I am in a position to do is give orders.”"},{"kind":"para","text":"That is the wrong way to take the work of the Lord. If you cannot be a part of an event you are planning from the Lord, you probably shouldn’t throw that event at all. Church leaders are not dictators. They are ministers, whether they like it or not. Ministers don’t tell people what to do. Ministers do the work that they want others to do too. They lead by example. At least they are supposed to."}]},{"start":6,"end":6,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This is actually a quote from the Lord Himself:"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"If ye love me, keep my commandments.","ref":"John 14:15"},{"kind":"para","text":"We have discussed how the commandments of Christ differ from the Ten Commandments, or moral law. The Ten Commandments lead us to Christ, while Christ’s commandments lead us to love. “IF” is the significant word in John 14:15. Jesus could have simply said, “Keep my commandments or else.” However, he does not say that. He was very specific in the way he pronounced this command. He simply says, “If you love me…”"},{"kind":"para","text":"The real question is, to every so-called “faithful” church leader today, do you love Christ? If you do, do you display that love to others? If you don’t display that love, you are not keeping Christ’s command to love one another:"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"A new commandment I give unto you, That ye love one another; as I have loved you, that ye also love one another.","ref":"John 13:34"},{"kind":"para","text":"I hate to break it to you, but if you are not keeping the command to love others, you do NOT love Christ."}]},{"start":7,"end":7,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Now, I’m not sure how easy it is for a “deceiver” to actually deceive someone who is a child of God. If you are truly walking in the “truth,” then it should be pretty hard for a man who openly admits to rejecting Christ to deceive you. But perhaps it’s easier than we thought."},{"kind":"para","text":"Christ tells us that these anti-Christs are really good at fooling people. He said they would come and pretend to be Him or would just straight up deny His existence. It will be so hard to tell (apparently) that they could even fool the elect if it were possible:"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"For there shall arise false Christs, and false prophets, and shall shew great signs and wonders; insomuch that, if it were possible, they shall deceive the very elect.","ref":"Matthew 24:24"},{"kind":"para","text":"John is point this out now. He knew they were going to come, but now he has seen it with his own eyes. Even in Acts they see someone mocking Christ:"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And when they had gone through the isle unto Paphos, they found a certain sorcerer, a false prophet, a Jew, whose name was Barjesus: Which was with the deputy of the country, Sergius Paulus, a prudent man; who called for Barnabas and Saul, and desired to hear the word of God. But Elymas the sorcerer (for so is his name by interpretation) withstood them, seeking to turn away the deputy from the faith. Then Saul, (who also is called Paul,) filled with the Holy Ghost, set his eyes on him, And said, O full of all subtilty and all mischief, thou child of the devil, thou enemy of all righteousness, wilt thou not cease to pervert the right ways of the Lord?","ref":"Acts 13:6-10"},{"kind":"para","text":"“Barjesus” means “son of Jesus.” Obviously, he was a false prophet, masquerading himself as something special in the name of Jesus. He was an anti-Christ. He had a lot of people fooled about his person, but it didn’t fool Paul. Paul knew it right away."},{"kind":"para","text":"I wonder how many Christians were there that were fooled by this man. I wonder how many Christians today are fooled by false Christs. It makes me sad to think about. What makes me more sad are the people who are doing the fooling, rather than the people being fooled. Rather, the people being fooled make me sad. The people doing the fooling make me angry."}]},{"start":8,"end":8,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Now, we might not know if a person is genuine or not (if they are an anti-Christ or not), but we should know ourselves for sure. We should be able to assess our own faith and know if we are walking in truth or believing in lies. If we cannot figure that out in ourselves, we can probably rightfully assure ourselves that we don’t have the truth of Christ. That needs to be fixed. John made that clear in the last chapter of 1 John. He said, “You can KNOW the Son, and you can KNOW you have salvation because of Him.”"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"He that hath the Son hath life; and he that hath not the Son of God hath not life. These things have I written unto you that believe on the name of the Son of God; that ye may know that ye have eternal life, and that ye may believe on the name of the Son of God.","ref":"1 John 5:12-13"},{"kind":"para","text":"It’s pretty straightforward if you think about it this way. It’s really not that complex. It’s also the best way to figure out if the person who is sitting across from you is of Christ or anti-Christ. Anyone who does not have Christ or believe in Christ as the Son of God (and as God in the flesh) is an anti-Christ. Period. That’s not me saying it; that’s John saying it."},{"kind":"para","text":"I know my name is John, but I did not write the scriptures. I just recite the scriptures. I’m nothing more than a humble messenger. If you have a problem with what John says here, you should probably take it up with him."}]},{"start":9,"end":9,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"It’s not just about rejecting Christ’s doctrine; it’s about not living in it at all. I think there are a lot of people in this world who actually agree with the doctrine of Christ. However, they will not live in it. It is required of a man to live in Christ’s doctrine."},{"kind":"para","text":"What is that doctrine? It is that Jesus was born of a virgin, lived a perfectly sinless life, died a cruel and undeserved death, and rose again three days later, proving His salvation. That’s the doctrine of Christ. There can’t be any other interpretation of it. You either live in that truth, or you don’t have God. That’s really what John is saying here. There’s no other interpretation of it, and I’m sorry if that offends you, but that is the truth of scripture written by the Holy Spirit of the Lord Himself."}]},{"start":10,"end":11,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This is a very interesting verse here. Obviously, reaching the lost is a very important venture for a child of God. It’s not the most important venture, but it’s certainly something that every child of God should be aware of and ready for. What John is speaking about here is not just people who don’t know or don’t believe like you. You will not run into many people who have faith like you do. Everyone is at a different level of faith. What is being said here is not about the people who simply don’t know or are just arrogant to the truth. Rather, John is warning against people who live opposed to “this doctrine” of Christ. They purposely want to shun the truth and want to convince the people of their lies. Those are the people who should not take up your time and space, especially if you are a leader of the church."},{"kind":"para","text":"I fear there are a lot of leaders today (inside and outside of the church) who have a lot of terrible voices surrounding them. They tell them that they should do this and that, when the ultimate goal for them is to cause division, confusion, and gain. They divide the truth, they confuse the people, and they rob the innocent for self-pleasure and evil purposes. Their end goal is to make the truth go unheard and for the unlearned to suffer. People like that have a field day in churches with many new members. I’ve seen churches grow at a rapid pace (which is fine), and many new people are coming into the fold (which is great), but the people that the leaders are listening to are people who do not have the congregation’s best interest in mind. Instead of the new people standing up for the faith and for the truth, and loving like they are supposed to, they just believe whatever the leaders say, and that causes them to fall away, and it causes the leaders to have a mess they will never be able to clean up on their own. Worse yet, they will get the church into a deep mess, with a bunch of baby Christians crying for help, and there is no way for them to meet all the needs. People get hurt, get swept and left behind, and the advice they listened to from deceitful people becomes a real problem for the church. This happens a lot today."},{"kind":"para","text":"The doctrine of Christ is very simple. If the people you are around (and taking advice from) are not keeping the doctrine of Christ simple, I promise you will end up hurting the most faithful and purposeful people in the faith. Those that are new to the faith won’t know the difference, and they will grow in the faith (arrogantly). They will all be led astray because they are innocent and don’t know any better. How sad and miserable a church leader (or any leader) must be to be so nonchalant in their care for those under them. I assume, however, that this lady that John is writing to was genuinely concerned about people who don’t follow the same faith coming in and seeking fellowship. That is why John is writing to her here. He is warning her and helping her lead the church in pureness and truth. Don’t let the lies of the anti-Christ trickle into the innocent members of the church."}]}]},{"start":12,"end":13,"title":"Being Present","entries":[{"start":12,"end":12,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"It’s better to talk to people face to face, even still today. I’ve met a lot of “ministers” in the churchhouse who only have relationships with people through their phones, even pastors, sadly. They don’t have time to speak to people face to face. Honestly, I could argue that the apostles had even less time to talk to people face to face. You know how far many of these people had to travel on foot to talk to someone face to face? We have cars, and we have the ability to see people from far distances all the time, and yet (somehow) we have less time for people than they did. The apostles had far more excuses to not talk to someone face to face. Whether it was because they were in prison for their faith, or because they were a two-week journey away for their faith, or they were taking some time to celebrate the Lord for their faith, or they were simply taking a break for their renewal of faith, they still made it a point to meet with people face to face. What is the modern church’s excuse? I think they have many excuses, but that is all they are. They are just excuses. They are not truths. Simply put, the only reason a minister may not meet with you face to face is not because they don’t have the time. It’s because (and I’m sorry to say) they don’t want to take the time. They are shunning their God-given necessity to meet with people face to face because they simply don’t want to minister to people’s needs. It has nothing to do with time or ability, and everything to do with laziness and desire."},{"kind":"para","text":"Personally (and I’m just being honest here), I don’t want to meet with people who are hurting. I don’t like to see people hurting. I don’t know many people who do. If you like to see someone being hurt, you are probably a psychopath. But we are called (as ministers) to meet with the hurting:"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And let us consider one another to provoke unto love and to good works: Not forsaking the assembling of ourselves together, as the manner of some is; but exhorting one another: and so much the more, as ye see the day approaching.","ref":"Hebrews 10:24-25"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"He that is not with me is against me; and he that gathereth not with me scattereth abroad.","ref":"Matthew 12:30"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"How is it then, brethren? when ye come together, every one of you hath a psalm, hath a doctrine, hath a tongue, hath a revelation, hath an interpretation. Let all things be done unto edifying.","ref":"1 Corinthians 14:26"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Gather my saints together unto me; those that have made a covenant with me by sacrifice.","ref":"Psalm 50:5"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"For God is not the author of confusion, but of peace, as in all churches of the saints.","ref":"1 Corinthians 14:33"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Wherefore comfort yourselves together, and edify one another, even as also ye do.","ref":"1 Thessalonians 5:11"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"As we have therefore opportunity, let us do good unto all men, especially unto them who are of the household of faith.","ref":"Galatians 6:10"},{"kind":"para","text":"All of these are about people in the church. We should be searching out the hurting in our own churches first before we visit the hurting in the world. How can we take care of the lost if we cannot take care of the saved properly first? We will not know how to truly love the lost if we cannot truly love the saints."}]},{"start":13,"end":13,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"I’m assuming that John is currently in a church that is part of the same faith or shares many of the same people. It might be a branched-out church, as there are many “satellite” churches today that have originated from larger ones. Perhaps that is how the church where John is at the moment started—it branched off from the church he is writing to. Either way, he is going to make an important effort to visit this church and tell them personally what needs to be done. He can’t just let letters replace his presence. No text message or letter should ever be able to replace someone’s presence, especially if you are a pastor in a church."}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":1},{"start":2,"end":2},{"start":3,"end":3},{"start":4,"end":4},{"start":5,"end":5},{"start":6,"end":6},{"start":7,"end":7},{"start":8,"end":8},{"start":9,"end":9},{"start":10,"end":11},{"start":12,"end":12},{"start":13,"end":13}],"verses":{"1":"The elder unto the elect lady and her children, whom I love in the truth; and not I only, but also all they that have known the truth;","2":"For the truth's sake, which dwelleth in us, and shall be with us for ever.","3":"Grace be with you, mercy, and peace, from God the Father, and from the Lord Jesus Christ, the Son of the Father, in truth and love.","4":"I rejoiced greatly that I found of thy children walking in truth, as we have received a commandment from the Father.","5":"And now I beseech thee, lady, not as though I wrote a new commandment unto thee, but that which we had from the beginning, that we love one another.","6":"And this is love, that we walk after his commandments. This is the commandment, That, as ye have heard from the beginning, ye should walk in it.","7":"For many deceivers are entered into the world, who confess not that Jesus Christ is come in the flesh. This is a deceiver and an antichrist.","8":"Look to yourselves, that we lose not those things which we have wrought, but that we receive a full reward.","9":"Whosoever transgresseth, and abideth not in the doctrine of Christ, hath not God. He that abideth in the doctrine of Christ, he hath both the Father and the Son.","10":"If there come any unto you, and bring not this doctrine, receive him not into your house, neither bid him God speed:","11":"For he that biddeth him God speed is partaker of his evil deeds.","12":"Having many things to write unto you, I would not write with paper and ink: but I trust to come unto you, and speak face to face, that our joy may be full.","13":"The children of thy elect sister greet thee. Amen."}},
  },
};
